{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Behold, I have given a thousand silver to your brother</b> — The sheep, cattle, and servants that he gave him were worth a thousand silver, and he said to Sarah: I have given great wealth to your brother. Behold, the silver is yours to cover the eyes of all who gaze at your beauty, for it will cover the eyes and heads of those who see, so they refrain from looking at you and from looking at all that is with you — even at your maidservants and handmaids. And behold, you were brought into my house for your own benefit, for they will fear you and cover their eyes from seeing you, saying: the king redeemed his life by sending his hand against the wife of the prophet. And Scripture informs us that he appeased Abraham with money and Sarah with words, so that he would not be punished through either of them. And it says that Sarah was not appeased by him, but despite all this she contended with him with arguments, so as not to forgive him — and Scripture spoke in her praise. But Abraham was appeased and prayed for him. This is like the language of \"and He will contend with Israel\" (Micah 6:2), \"surely I will set my ways before Him and argue\" (Job 13:15), \"there the upright will reason with Him\" (Job 23:7). And it is possible that \"a thousand silver\" means thousands of silver — great wealth, as befits a king. Similarly, \"the least shall become a thousand\" (Isaiah 60:22) — a great multitude. And similarly, \"Restore to them this very day their fields, their vineyards, their olive groves, their houses, and the hundred silver and the grain, the wine, and the oil that you exact from them\" (Nehemiah 5:11) — hundreds upon hundreds, a great sum."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:32:25.501861909Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}